### What happened

`getRequestListener`'s request-drain logic force-closes the socket after `DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS` when the incoming body was not fully read. `forceClose` assumes the socket is a real `net.Socket`:

```js
const forceClose = () => {
  cleanup();
  const socket = incoming.socket;
  if (socket && !socket.destroyed) {
    socket.destroySoon();
  }
};
```

When `incoming` is not a real `http.IncomingMessage` (for example a mocked request), `socket.destroyed` is `undefined` (so the guard passes) and `socket.destroySoon` does not exist, producing an **uncaught exception on a timer** that callers cannot catch:

```
TypeError: socket.destroySoon is not a function
 ❯ Timeout.forceClose node_modules/@hono/node-server/dist/index.mjs:390:14
 ❯ listOnTimeout node:internal/timers:585:17
 ❯ processTimers node:internal/timers:521:7
```

### How we hit this

The MCP TypeScript SDK (`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk`, `StreamableHTTPServerTransport`) uses `getRequestListener` to bridge Node req/res to fetch Request/Response. We mount that transport inside a Fastify route and integration-test it with `fastify.inject()` (light-my-request). light-my-request's `MockSocket` is a plain `EventEmitter` with neither `destroyed` nor `destroySoon`, so every injected MCP request schedules a 500 ms timer that later crashes the (vitest) worker with the unhandled `TypeError` above. Because it fires on a timer, it is timing-dependent and shows up as flaky "Unhandled Errors" in otherwise green test runs.

### Environment

- `@hono/node-server` 1.19.14 (via `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` 1.29.0)
- Node v22.23.0
- light-my-request 6.6.0 (fastify inject)

### Suggested fix

Make `forceClose` defensive about non-standard sockets:

```js
const forceClose = () => {
  cleanup();
  const socket = incoming.socket;
  if (socket && !socket.destroyed) {
    if (typeof socket.destroySoon === 'function') {
      socket.destroySoon();
    } else {
      socket.destroy?.();
    }
  }
};
```

That keeps the drain behaviour for real sockets and turns the mocked-socket case into a no-op instead of an uncatchable crash. Happy to send a PR if that direction works for you.

### What version of `@hono/node-server` are you using?

2.1.1 (with `hono` 4.13.8, Node v24.19.0, macOS)

### What is the problem?

When a handler passes a plain object as the third argument of `c.body()`, the adapter writes `Content-Length` **into that object**. If the object is reused across responses — a module-level constant, for example — the next response fails with `TypeError: v is not iterable` and the server returns 500.

So the first request succeeds and every subsequent one fails, which is an unpleasant failure mode to debug: it looks like a caching or concurrency problem rather than a mutated constant.

### Minimal reproduction

```js
import { serve } from '@hono/node-server';
import { Hono } from 'hono';

// A header object reused across responses, e.g. a module-level constant.
const HEADERS = { 'content-type': 'text/plain; charset=utf-8' };

const app = new Hono();
app.get('/', (c) => c.body(new Uint8Array([104, 105]), 200, HEADERS));

serve({ fetch: app.fetch, port: 3999 }, async () => {
  for (let i = 1; i <= 2; i++) {
    const res = await fetch('http://127.0.0.1:3999/');
    console.log(`request ${i}: ${res.status}`);
  }
  console.log("the caller's object is now:", JSON.stringify(HEADERS));
  process.exit(0);
});
```

Output:

```
request 1: 200
TypeError: v is not iterable
request 2: 500
the caller's object is now: {"content-type":"text/plain; charset=utf-8","Content-Length":2}
```

### Cause

In `src/listener.ts`, when `header` is a plain object the `Content-Length` is assigned directly to it:

https://github.com/honojs/node-server/blob/main/src/listener.ts#L228-L235

```ts
if (!hasContentLength) {
  if (typeof body === 'string') {
    header['Content-Length'] = Buffer.byteLength(body)
  } else if (body instanceof Uint8Array) {
    header['Content-Length'] = body.byteLength
  } else if (body instanceof Blob) {
    header['Content-Length'] = body.size
  }
}
```

The `Headers` and array branches just above build a new object via `buildOutgoingHttpHeaders`, so only the plain-object path mutates its input. Since Hono keeps the caller's object as the response's header init, that write lands on the object the handler owns.

The 500 on the following request then comes from Hono's side: `Context.#newResponse` walks the header record and treats any non-string value as an iterable of values, and `Content-Length` is now a **number**:

```js
if (typeof v === 'string') responseHeaders.set(k, v)
else { responseHeaders.delete(k); for (const v2 of v) responseHeaders.append(k, v2) }   // ← v is a number
```

### Why it is easy to miss

`app.request()` never goes through this adapter, so a test suite that exercises handlers in-process passes cleanly while production fails from the second request onward. In our case the bug reached a deployed service with full coverage of the affected route.

### Suggested fix

Copy before writing, so the caller's object is left as it was:

```ts
if (!hasContentLength) {
  const length =
    typeof body === 'string' ? Buffer.byteLength(body)
    : body instanceof Uint8Array ? body.byteLength
    : body instanceof Blob ? body.size
    : undefined
  if (length !== undefined) {
    header = { ...header, 'Content-Length': length }
  }
}
```

Using a string value would also be more consistent with the rest of the header record, though the copy is the part that fixes the reuse case.

Happy to open a PR if the approach looks right.

## Summary

If the client disconnects while an async handler is still running, the `Response` body the handler then returns is never cancelled. The adapter reads one or two chunks and then waits on a closed socket forever. The stream's `cancel()` never runs, so whatever is producing the body (an upstream `fetch`, an SSE generator, a DB cursor) is held until the process exits.

Reproduces on `@hono/node-server` 1.19.14 and 2.1.5 (latest), with `hono` 4.13.13 on Node v22.23.2.

## Reproduction

`package.json`

```json
{ "type": "module", "dependencies": { "@hono/node-server": "2.1.5", "hono": "4.13.13" } }
```

`repro.mjs`

```js
import { createServer, request } from "node:http";
import { once } from "node:events";
import { getRequestListener } from "@hono/node-server";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
const pkg = JSON.parse(readFileSync("node_modules/@hono/node-server/package.json", "utf8"));

let cancelled = false;
let closed;
const socketClosed = new Promise((resolve) => (closed = resolve));

const server = createServer(
  getRequestListener(async () => {
    await socketClosed;
    return new Response(
      new ReadableStream({
        start(controller) {
          controller.enqueue(new TextEncoder().encode("data: first\n\n"));
        },
        cancel() {
          cancelled = true;
        },
      }),
      { headers: { "content-type": "text/event-stream" } },
    );
  }),
);
server.on("request", (_req, res) => res.once("close", closed));
server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1");
await once(server, "listening");

const client = request(`http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/`);
client.on("error", () => {});
client.end();
setTimeout(() => client.destroy(), 50);
await socketClosed;
await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 1000));
console.log(`@hono/node-server ${pkg.version} on Node ${process.version}: body cancelled = ${cancelled}`);
server.closeAllConnections();
server.close();
```

```
$ npm i && node repro.mjs
@hono/node-server 2.1.5 on Node v22.23.2: body cancelled = false
```

The same script on 1.19.14 prints `body cancelled = false` too.

**Expected:** `body cancelled = true`. The socket is already closed, so the body should be cancelled, as it is when the disconnect happens after streaming starts.

## Cause

[`writeFromReadableStreamDefaultReader`](https://github.com/honojs/node-server/blob/4b47a0d8a5c12ed69a9f8493df69918f62fb61c0/src/utils.ts#L10-L51) attaches its `close`/`error` listeners to cancel the reader. If `outgoing` was already destroyed before the response arrived, `close` has already fired and never fires again. After that, either the pending `reader.read()` never settles (the producer is waiting for a consumer), or `writable.write()` returns `false` and `once('drain')` waits forever.

The same gap exists in a narrow window after the response arrives on a live socket: [`responseViaResponseObject`](https://github.com/honojs/node-server/blob/4b47a0d8a5c12ed69a9f8493df69918f62fb61c0/src/listener.ts#L364-L410) may `await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve))` between its first reads and attaching those listeners. A disconnect inside that tick is missed in the same way.

`writeFromReadableStream` already returns early for `writable.destroyed`, but it doesn't cancel the stream, and the `responseViaResponseObject` path doesn't go through that check.

## Suggested fix

In `writeFromReadableStreamDefaultReader`, after attaching the listeners, cancel immediately if `writable.destroyed` (or `writable.closed`) is already true. Optionally, `responseViaResponseObject` could skip the body reads and cancel `res.body` when `outgoing.destroyed` is true on entry.

## Related, but separate

- #388 covers the reason passed to `cancel()` on `close`. Here `cancel()` is never called at all.
- In 1.19.x, a request `signal` first accessed after the disconnect also never aborts, because the controller is created lazily. 2.x fixes that half, but not the body.

## Workaround

A first middleware that reads `c.req.raw.signal` on entry (so 1.x creates the controller), and after `await next()` calls `c.res.body?.cancel(signal.reason)` if `signal.aborted`. This covers a disconnect before the handler returns, but not the `setTimeout` window above.

